if you want to be more explosive more  agile faster more powerful then you need  to train your feet as well as your legs  I've already discussed in the past how  using dorsiflexion can help you to jump  higher that means you're starting with  your toes pointing upwards and then that  way having further to push your feet  down into the ground generating more  momentum and more power when you drive  the balls of the feet into the ground to  push yourself up but even without this  added step you're always using the toe  flexors when you push off the ground  training things like calf jumps will  naturally be using our toe flexor  muscles a little bit one example is the  flexor digitorum longus this is a deep  muscle of the leg that goes from the  calf specifically the tibialis posterior  found near the soleus and attaches to  the four small toes these are pretty  important for things like supporting the  arch of the foot which we've previously  discussed is crucial for engaging the  glutes to make yourself more glue to  dominance and more powerful remember the  glutes are the number one anti gravity  muscles in your body  the other important toe flexor muscle is  the flexor hallucis longus this is the  muscle that connects to the big toe and  is one of the driving forces when it  comes to pushing off the floor the way  we can think of these flexor muscles is  as a kind of bridge between the powerful  muscles of the leg and the ground in  other words you can have the most  powerful glutes and hamstrings and quads  and curves in the world but if you have  weak feet then you can't translate that  power into the ground to be as explosive  as possible improving mobility here is  also very important because if your  ankles are stiff then you can't move  your feet as freely to push yourself so  it's really important you learn to use  all these muscles in order to drive  yourself forwards and into the air as  well as to maintain proper stance and  form  studies show that toe flexor muscles do  respond to training and that this can  significantly increase jump height  distance and directional change our feet  toes and ankles have other important  roles in our performance to our toes can  splay in order to prevent us twisting  our ankle or tripping over when we land  on uneven terrain at the same time ankle  mobility allows us to push off the  ground at different angles and still  achieve a huge amount of force so of  course they play a big role when it  comes to balance and they provide us  with proprioceptive feedback about the  ground underneath us  you can even grip onto the ground using  our toes in order to gain more traction  and to push off more powerfully when  running so how do we make sure that  we're training the toe flexors and  feet in general well one tip is to make  sure the footwear you're using is able  to properly support full range of motion  I like my vivo barefoot the vibram nips  which I recently got another example is  this they use shoe which I believe is  worn by IDO portal these remove that big  thick wedge of sole that normally sits  between your foot and the ground thereby  allowing you to actually use your feet  in a useful manner to translate strength  from specific toes into the ground to  feel the ground underneath you you need  to ease yourself into this don't get a  pair of barefoot shoes right away it  starts printing but gradually look for  shoes with a thinner and thinner sole  less and less of a heel to toe drop and  a bigger toe box that way you can build  the strength gradually to avoid injury  better yet of course is to train  barefoot and that way you can really  engage your big toe in the movements  utilize the mind muscle connection to  make sure that it's involved as it  should be when squatting and jumping  again you need to be very careful easing  your way into barefoot running and the  like but you can slightly more safely  slip your shoes off if you're training  at home in your gym I'd be remiss of  course not to mention the Tarahumara  tribe who famously run huge distances  daily and part of the reason that we  think they're able to do this is because  they run in very minimal Footwear or  entirely barefoot finally if you want to  train the big toe specifically or the  smaller toes then you can do so using a  resistance band sit on the ground and  wrap the band around the toe as though  you're about to pull it into the  dorsiflexion position as you do that  gently push against the resistance with  your big toe or the for smaller toes by  flexing this works really well to  isolate those muscles but I do find that  most people are not going to have the  time to dedicate to specific foot  training like this on top of everything  else which is why I like this other  option more so ballet dancers are of  course very well known for their  impressive foot strength and there are  various different types of training they  use to develop this which will almost  certainly be the basis for a future  video one of my favorites though is  simply practicing pushing with the big  toe into the floor and holding that as a  static contraction so you can do this  anywhere you could be sitting down it  could be waiting in the queue and just  consciously push that big toe into the  ground and you can also practice  alternating between the big toe and the  four others whilst doing this or even  making fists with the toes  finally try simply going as high onto  your toes as possible and then holding  the position that's something else  do throughout the day as a form of  incidental training and of course you  should also think about the role of your  toes when you're performing any other  movement like a squat or a lunge just  make sure you're really working that  mind-muscle connection and thinking  about even this extremity and what it's  doing whilst you're moving through that  motion so if you want to see even better  gains from your explosive legs workouts  then add these exercises to the end of  routines or in and around the rest of  your training so it was just a quick tip  I hope you found that useful and  interesting let me know if I've missed  anything interesting about foot training  I'm sure I'll talk about the hyperox in  future but any other topics I'd love to  hear about I really love it when you  give me suggestions for future videos  I've had loads of great ideas some of my  best videos have been suggested by you  guys and I really appreciate it  so yeah I've got lots more like this on  the way thanks a ton for watching and  I'll see you next time bye for now  [Music]
 

